ique  futuristic settings and the fallout  Miami team is dipping into Miami's  history in order to give it a believable  spin Miami itself wasn't hit by a nuke  but it sounds like the Cuban Missile  Crisis and Bay of Pigs could play an  important role in fallout Miami's  apocalyptic history however this being a  fallout game don't expect these events  to play out as they do in a textbook  wanna fall at Miami's project leaders  describes the Bay of Pigs as a  combined-arms operation complete with  laser weapons and power armor used to  push the Chinese out of Cuba well it  sounds like you won't be able to take  part in the battle you will hear a lot  about it as you explore if you've kept  an eye on the modding community over the  last decade or so you've probably seen  similar projects like this pop up  projects that garner a lot of attention  go quiet for years and either fizzle out  or if they're lucky release years later  a prime example of a large-scale mob  that crossed the finish line was project  Brasil also known as fallout new  California the project was  conceptualized in 2010 and released late  last year however mods like fallout new  California or Beyond Skyrim's Bruma are  the exception not the standard so let's  say despite the odds stacked against you  you decide to move forward with your  large-scale mod you design a map and  create a loose narrative next thing  you'll need is to recruit a team luckily  you've been entrenched in the modding  scene for quite some time now and you  know some killer 3d artists some crack  level designers and a couple of sharp  writers to join your project your team  is knocking out production tasks and  create  assets at a steady rate with that  creative burst of energy you decide to  expand you and your team put together a  trailer and upload it to YouTube  overnight it goes viral and when you  wake up you have hundreds of  applications in your inbox with a team  of this size you think you can knock out  your mod in no time k Constantine and  sir Balin to project leads of Fallout  Miami had a similar experience  after the team released a trailer for  their mod follow up Miami was first  conceptualized some time in 2016 by a  modder who goes by Myka 999 he began  working on a world space but never  really expected it to expand as much as  it did as the team was starting to ramp  up production they needed to expand  their team this is a common practice for  mod teams and it's usually why we see  trailers for mods so early when a studio  or publisher releases a trailer it's to  build hype when a large-scale mod team  releases a trailer it's usually because  they need to increase their headcount  that was actually one of the points of  the trailer and why we were pushing  ourselves to actually get it out we knew  that we have a really strong aesthetic  that people appreciate if they can see  it so we've needed people to see it then  but a lot of people's heart however more  people can lead to more problems it's  easy to keep track of a team of five  people keeping track of 25 to 50 people  is another story without a clear roadmap  a concise workflow and some management a  mod project can get derailed rather  quickly the last thing you want is for  your volunteers to be doubling up on  work or chipping away it's something  that got cut weeks ago in terms of a  project with something close to like 100  people that have contributed so far and  a pretty sizable active team you need a  sort of project management experience to  get things done and more goal oriented  and more corner oriented approach in an  office if you saw a team member working  on something that was cut you probably  tapped him on the shoulder and let them  know or maybe you'd hold an all-hands  meeting where you can share your updated  vision however with the team scattered  across different countries at different  time zones you can't really do that  another issue that can arise from a big  team is an ever-expanding scope being on  the outside it's easy to think oh they  have a much bigger team now that means  they can make the mod even bigger  according to Kay Constantine this is a  recipe for disaster and it's why fallout  Miami's size has largely  remain the same the scope didn't really  expand much at all because one thing  that is sort of a constant largemont  projects like this is that they don't  release things like new California are  very much the exception these things  just don't become a reality and one of  the problems with that is feature creep  in the cost of the expanding scope and  we wanted to keep things controlled you  want to kind of plan and stick to it but  that begs the question exactly how big  is fallout Miami supposed to be  according to Kay Constantine it should  be slightly bigger than fallout 4s  Bethesda developed DLC far Harbor the  world will be slightly bigger there will  be slightly more characters and this  story will be slightly longer the  restraint the team is showing might  disappoint some fans but like I said  before it's important to remember that  most of these large-scale mods never see  the light of day if anything that  restraint and focus is a promising sign  that fallout Miami will cross the finish  line the only thing that's happening is  the sort of the time horizon for how  long it might take to get this finished  has shrunk incredible so you've built up  your team you have a perfect roadmap and  an airtight workflow everyone knows  exactly what they should be doing and  when it should be done by but then your  lead level designer finds a job at a  triple-a studio your 3d artists are  buried in school work and your lead  writer is getting married and doesn't  have as much time to commit to the  project we've had people who show up  contribute on one assets were an art  asset and then had they have to leave if  we had people who joined the team and  actually don't even finish their first  project and have to leave we've had  people that join instead of state would  be team pretty much constantly since  they've joined after years of refining  and developing your vision with these  people they need to move on leaving many  of their projects half done the release  of your mod seemed far enough away as is  but now the goal posts have been moved  even further back not only do you have  to find replacements for all those open  positions but you have to catch everyone  up on years of work  while turnover can be a looming fear  when it comes to large-scale mods most  of the modders I spoke to didn't seem  all that worried a bigger challenge was  motivating and organizing volunteers who  have a fluctuating schedule and can only  commit a few hours a week to the project  however another fallout mod I took a  look at has an interesting history with  turnover  why the mod was first conceptualized  shortly after fallout 4 released by a  modern named Alex Cascadia takes place  in and around Seattle based on the  trailers and gameplay the team is shown  it's shaping up to be a vibrant and  imaginative take on the Fallout universe  that not only aims to expand on fallout  4 but take some of the Fallout series  best aspects and inject them into  Fallout 4s engine the team is  implementing a redone dialogue interface  that ditches the limiting dialogue wheel  and they are aiming to revive fallout  threes perk system a Mike Miami Cascadia  has almost nothing to do with fallout 4  settings story or plot the mod takes  place years after the events of four you  play as the drifter your backstory as  the drifter is more or less a clean  slate I sat down with the former Project  Lead Flynn Arne and the current Project  Lead Leon both gave me a similar  impression of the mod ambitious even  though leadership has changed hands a  few times it sounds like the team has a  clear vision of what fallout Cascadia  should and will look like however taking  on the role of Project Lead it wasn't an  easy transition the first day I became  Project Lead I woke up and I literally  had a hundred I had a hundred messages  waiting for me moment I woke up I'm just  like oh boy fortunately things are going  a lot smoother now however his sleep  schedule has been interesting part of  this is due to the fact that his team is  scattered across the world there isn't a  time that works for everyone and that  means early mornings and late nights so  he can coordinate with his team and for  a mod this big coordination is key  according to Leon the team plans to  deliver a map that is roughly twice the  size of Fallout fours in fact the shoe  size of Cascadia has caused a number of  issues on its own one in particular had  to do with version control which is away  modders and developers compile assets to  push out a build so we have this issue  where whatever we did we could add  nothing else to the file the creation  kit would not tell us anything it would  not air out it would just not let you  add anything and we actually had this  issue for about a month and a half if I  recall correctly where there were a  couple of us that were trying to work  behind the scenes trying to figure out  what was  going on and we just could not figure it  out at all and it's because our world  space is so high that we have a lot of a  lot more objects so I guess this isn't  really an issue that people would catch  but there's a bug with the creation kit  where once you hit a certain amount of  objects in the file it just will refuse  to let you add anymore  fortunately Leon in the Cascadia team  managed to get to the bottom of this  issue on the other hand Leon didn't seem  all that worried about turnover it  happens but it's not always as  detrimental as it could be even if  someone gets halfway through a project  before dropping off they can usually  find someone to pick up the slack  turnover with writers though can be a  bit more complicated when it comes to  writing that's really annoying because  then chances are it doesn't exactly fit  with whoever takes on that task next is  vision and then we they usually just  decide to redo it completely  [Music]  no matter how prepared you are there are  always going to be fires you will need  to put out and at the end of the day  investing hours and hours into a mod  usually won't pay the bills so why do  these modders dedicate their lives to  projects like these well for some it's  simply a hobby it's something creative  to do after work or class that builds  camaraderie others contribute to  projects like these to bolster their  portfolio and hopefully land a paid  position at a studio some do it because  they love fallout and want to put their  own unique spin on that world some do it  for all three when I asked Liana  Constantin why they spend day in and day  out working on their mods they gave me  similar answers they loved the Fallout  series and they wanted to make something  that fans would enjoy so when do these  mods come out you probably know what I'm  gonna say but I have no idea the teams  had rough estimates as to when the  public might get their hands on these  projects but anything can happen and  with all things considered I think the  very least we can do for these modders  is to be patient like you I am beyond  excited to get my hands on Miami in  Cascadia both mods are shaping up to be  an exciting take on the modern Fallout  series and both teams have something  interesting and unique they are bringing  to the table however I think it's  important to highlight the amount of  work dedication and sacrifice that comes  with projects like these even the mods  that fizzle out live on in some way or  another lessons learned from some of  these mods are taken and applied  elsewhere and sometimes assets levels  and characters are reworked for another  mod and in some cases someone with a  fresh perspective might pick up the  torch for a dormant large-scale mod and  push the project a little further thank  you all for watching this video there  are a couple of people I want to thank  for helping make this possible the  follow up Miami team the fall Cascadia  team K constant surveillance to project  leads behind Miami Leon and Flynn our  new Leon is the current lead of project  Cascadia and Flynn Arne was the previous  one who I interviewed at first and then  Cape Alto draws who made the thumbnail  for this video you should check out her  artwork if you get the chance and  finally all the music you heard in this  video are from fallout Miami and fallout  Cascadia so make sure to check out those  tracks on youtube if you did enjoy them  thank you  [Music]

